<% content_for :title, 'Star system registration' %>

<%= render 'help/page', title: 'Star System Registration' do %>
  <div class='max-w-2xl space-y-6'>
    <div class='rounded-2xl border border-slate-800/70 bg-slate-950/50 p-5 space-y-6'>
      <div class='space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-300'>
        <p>
          The registration form creates a new star system in a selected parsec hex.
          Enter a name, choose a hex (if not already selected from context), and pick
          one of three initialisation modes. Press <span class='font-semibold text-slate-200'>Commit Core</span> to create the system.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class='text-sm font-semibold text-slate-200 mb-2'>Initialization modes</p>

        <dl class='space-y-5 text-sm'>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-slate-200'>Empty</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-slate-300'>
              Creates a star system with no orbital bodies. When this mode is selected you
              configure the primary star by choosing a spectral type, subtype,
              and luminosity class. A primary star is required for a star system.
            </dd>
            <dd class='mt-2 text-slate-300'>
              Use this when you have existing star systems you want added to the app.
            </dd>
          </div>

          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-slate-200'>Random</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-slate-300'>
              This option generates a fully populated star system with stars, orbital bodies, and a main world. No
              additional input is needed.
            </dd>
            <dd class='mt-2 text-slate-300'>
              Use this when you want a complete system quickly and don't need control
              over its composition.
            </dd>
          </div>

          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-slate-200'>Build configuration</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-slate-300'>
              Accepts a YAML configuration that tells the generator how to construct the system.
              You can specify the primary star and its companions, orbital body counts or
              density, allegiance, bases, and individual orbital bodies with specific UWP codes.
            </dd>
            <dd class='mt-2 text-slate-300'>
              Use this when you need a generated system but want to constrain some of its
              properties. See the
              <%= link_to 'star system build specification',
                          help_star_system_build_specification_path,
                          class: 'hover:underline underline-offset-4' %>
              for the full YAML reference.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class='text-sm font-semibold text-slate-200 mb-2'>Primary star fields (Empty mode)</p>

        <dl class='space-y-5 text-sm'>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-slate-200'>Type</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-slate-300'>
              The spectral type of the star: O, B, A, F, G, K, or M. Special types
              (Brown Dwarf, White Dwarf, Black Hole, Pulsar, Neutron Star) are also available.
            </dd>
          </div>

          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-slate-200'>Subtype</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-slate-300'>
              A digit from 0 to 9 that refines the spectral type. Lower numbers are
              hotter within the class. Not required for special types.
            </dd>
          </div>

          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-slate-200'>Luminosity class</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-slate-300'>
              The size classification of the star, from Ia (luminous supergiant)
              through VI (subdwarf). Main sequence stars are class V. Not required
              for special types.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class='pt-2 text-right uppercase font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.22em] text-slate-500'>
        REFERENCE 0423RD
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
<% end %>